 the uncorroborated word of one  narcotics officer so that was a big part  of the story is why did they let him  operate in that manner to begin with and  then why didn't they question the  quality of the cases  when they started to come back in after  no cocaine had been confiscated during  the roundup after he had no second  officer corroborating his story um and  then the sentences were what really got  me into the story because they were just  amazing the first man was convicted of  dealing 200 worth of cocaine he got 90  years  another young man got 361 years again  for just a series of small deliveries  and even some of the defendants with no  prior records were getting the maximum  sentence  was there a race component to this story  that was one of the red flags that that  immediately stood out about this story  that the town of tula is only about five  percent black um but 39 of the 47 people  he indicted were african-american and so  if you actually broke down the math he  was accusing one in five black adults in  this tiny little ranching town of  dealing powdered cocaine it was it was  absurd on the face of it and yet they  pushed through the prosecutions anyway  you covered this story for the texas  observer did your coverage uh have  something to do with the reversal of the  convictions well our we did the first  investigative story in the observer and  that led to a a major uh breaking of the  story it was re-reported for the new  york times the l.a times cnn and then it  just became a huge national scandal  just just the infuriating thing was  though that even after everybody agreed  it was a huge scandal and laws have been  passed here at the capitol to make sure  it never happened again you still had  two dozen people that were in prison and  very little going on to get them out and  so they they literally sat in prison um  up to four years in some cases until  finally this this post-conviction  process got going and that's what i  chronicle in the 